  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why do lorries use air instead of brake fluid?",
      "a": "Air is free and never runs out, a small leak does not leave you without brakes straight away, and air lines are easy to connect between a lorry and its trailer.",
      "asks": [
        "Why don't lorries use hydraulic brakes?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the air tank for?",
      "a": "The air tanks, called reservoirs, store compressed air so there is enough for many brake applications even if the compressor stops. A gauge warns the driver if pressure falls.",
      "asks": [
        "How much air does a lorry store?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do lorries hiss when they stop?",
      "a": "When the driver releases the brake, the valve lets the used air out of the brake chambers into the open with a loud hiss. The compressor then tops the tanks up.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the hissing noise from a bus?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a brake chamber?",
      "a": "The brake chamber is a round metal can at each wheel with a rubber diaphragm inside. Air pressure pushes the diaphragm and a rod, which turns a lever to apply the brake.",
      "asks": [
        "How does air push the brakes on?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens if a lorry loses air pressure?",
      "a": "Strong springs in the brake chambers apply the brakes when air pressure falls, so a burst pipe or a trailer breaking away makes the brakes come on rather than fail.",
      "asks": [
        "Are air brakes fail-safe?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "George Westinghouse patented the railway air brake in 1869.",
    "Lorry air brake systems typically store air at about eight to ten times atmospheric pressure.",
    "Spring brakes on lorries apply automatically if air pressure is lost."
  ],
